{
 "slug": "vector-operations",
 "title": "Vector Operations",
 "seo_title": "Vector Operations Worksheet: Addition, Scalars, Unit Vectors, Dot Product | 100 Questions",
 "short_title": "Vector Operations",
 "domain": "Vectors and Matrices",
 "standard_code": "N-VM.B.4",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/HSN/VM/B/4/",
 "standard_text": "Add and subtract vectors. Multiply a vector by a scalar.",
 "meta_desc": "Free precalculus worksheet on vector operations: adding and subtracting, scalar multiples, unit vectors, i and j notation, the dot product, angles and resultant forces. 100 questions.",
 "key_big": "Work component by component.",
 "key_sub": "Unit vector: divide by the magnitude. Dot product 0 means perpendicular.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>Add, subtract and multiply by a scalar <b>component by component</b>: ⟨<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>⟩ + ⟨<i>c</i>, <i>d</i>⟩ = ⟨<i>a</i> + <i>c</i>, <i>b</i> + <i>d</i>⟩ and <i>k</i>⟨<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>⟩ = ⟨<i>ka</i>, <i>kb</i>⟩.</li><li>A <b>unit vector</b> has length 1: divide a vector by its magnitude. <b><i>i</i></b> = ⟨1, 0⟩ and <b><i>j</i></b> = ⟨0, 1⟩, so ⟨<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>⟩ = <i>a</i><b><i>i</i></b> + <i>b</i><b><i>j</i></b>.</li><li><b>Dot product</b>: ⟨<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>⟩ · ⟨<i>c</i>, <i>d</i>⟩ = <i>ac</i> + <i>bd</i>, a number. The angle between vectors: cos θ = (<b><i>u</i></b> · <b><i>v</i></b>) / (‖<b><i>u</i></b>‖‖<b><i>v</i></b>‖). Dot product 0 means perpendicular.</li><li>The <b>resultant</b> of forces or velocities is their vector sum. Add components, then find the magnitude.</li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>If <b><i>u</i></b> = ⟨3, −2⟩ and <b><i>v</i></b> = ⟨−1, 4⟩, find 2<b><i>u</i></b> − 3<b><i>v</i></b>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<b><i>u</i></b> = ⟨6, −4⟩, 3<b><i>v</i></b> = ⟨−3, 12⟩</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>⟨6 − (−3), −4 − 12⟩</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: ⟨9, −16⟩</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find the angle between ⟨1, 0⟩ and ⟨1, 1⟩.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Dot product 1</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>cos θ = 1/(1 · √2)</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 45°</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying only the first component by the scalar.</li><li>Dividing by <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> instead of the magnitude to make a unit vector.</li><li>Writing the dot product as a vector. It is a single number.</li><li>Adding the magnitudes of forces that are not in the same direction.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you add vectors?",
   "a": "Add the matching components: ⟨a, b⟩ + ⟨c, d⟩ = ⟨a + c, b + d⟩. Geometrically, place them tip to tail."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find a unit vector?",
   "a": "Divide the vector by its magnitude. For ⟨3, 4⟩, the unit vector is ⟨3/5, 4/5⟩."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does the dot product tell you?",
   "a": "It gives the angle between two vectors through cos θ = (u · v)/(|u||v|). A dot product of 0 means the vectors are perpendicular."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a resultant force?",
   "a": "The single force equal to the vector sum of all the forces acting on an object."
  }
 ],
